.TH CHECKSSL 1 "2026-10-12" "checkssl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
checkssl \- inspect TLS certificates for one or more domains
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B checkssl
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B checkssl completion
.RI { bash | zsh | fish }
.br
.B checkssl config
.RI { path | edit | show | init | migrate | gc }
.RI [ flags ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B checkssl
opens a TLS connection to each listed domain, retrieves the peer
certificate, and reports its expiry date and health. Expired,
self-signed, and hostname-mismatched certificates are reported as
data \(em not as connection errors \(em so the tool can distinguish
between "cert is bad" and "server is down".
.PP
Handshakes run concurrently (default 100 in parallel). The exit code
reflects certificate health, making
.B checkssl
suitable as a cron probe or Nagios/Icinga plugin.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-domain " " \fIDOMAIN\fR
Check a specific domain. May be given multiple times.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Read one domain per line from
.IR FILE .
Lines beginning with
.RB \(oq # \(cq
and blank lines are ignored;
.RB \(oq # \(cq
also starts an end-of-line comment. May be given multiple times.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-silent
Suppress the error summary written to stderr.
.TP
.BR \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format. One of
.BR table " (default), " csv ", " json ", " nagios ", " html .
See
.B OUTPUT FORMATS
below.
.TP
.BR \-\-concurrency " " \fIN\fR
Maximum parallel TLS handshakes. Default 100.
.TP
.BR \-\-timeout " " \fIDURATION\fR
Per-domain handshake timeout, in Go duration syntax
.RI ( e.g.\ 5s ,
.IR 500ms ).
Default 5s.
.TP
.BR \-\-nagios\-warning " " \fIDAYS\fR
Warn threshold in days for
.BR "\-\-format nagios" .
Default 30.
.TP
.BR \-\-nagios\-critical " " \fIDAYS\fR
Critical threshold in days for
.BR "\-\-format nagios" .
Default 14.
.TP
.BR \-\-only " " \fISTATUS,...\fR
Comma-separated list of status buckets to keep in the output. Rows
with any other status are hidden. Accepted values:
.BR valid ", " expiring_soon ", " expired ", " invalid ", " error
(hyphens are accepted too, e.g.
.BR expiring\-soon ).
Changes the exit code: 1 when at least one row matches the filter,
0 when none match. This makes
.B checkssl
a self-silencing cron probe:
.PP
.RS
.EX
checkssl -f ~/.checkssl --only error,invalid,expired \\
    || curl -d "$(checkssl -f ~/.checkssl --only error,invalid,expired)" ntfy
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B \-\-format nagios
owns its own exit code and ignores this behaviour.
.TP
.BR \-\-show\-ip
Include the resolved IP address in the table, CSV, JSON and HTML
output. For the table a new column is inserted between
.B Domain
and
.BR Expiration .
CSV gains an
.B IP
column after
.BR Domain .
JSON gains a
.B resolvedIP
field. The IP shown is whichever address the TLS handshake actually
connected over \(em with IPv4 preferred.
.BR "\-\-format nagios"
ignores this flag.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-tui
Launch the interactive terminal UI instead of printing a one-shot
report. Shows the scan as it runs, with keyboard-driven navigation,
filtering, sorting, and in-place edits of the source config files.
Keys:
.BR j / k
navigate,
.BR g / G
jump to first/last,
.BR l / Enter
details panel,
.B e
open the configured editor on the source file at the right line,
.B #
comment out the selected line,
.B D
delete the selected line (needs
.B y
to confirm),
.B N
create a new include-list file and open it in the editor,
.B ,
open
.IR config.toml
in the editor,
.BR s / S
cycle sort column,
.B d
toggle sort direction,
.BR 1 - 5
toggle status buckets,
.BR /
filter,
.BR r
re-scan,
.BR ?
help,
.BR q
quit.
.IP
External edits to the watched config files (vim, VSCode, …) trigger
an automatic re-scan via
.BR fsnotify (3).
In-TUI mutations snapshot the file into
.I <cache dir>/backups/<session timestamp>/
before writing.
.BR "checkssl config gc"
prunes old snapshots.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print version.
.SH "CONFIGURATION"
.B checkssl
keeps its files in the OS-native user config directory:
.IR ~/.config/checkssl/
on Linux,
.IR "~/Library/Application Support/checkssl/"
on macOS,
.IR %AppData%\\\\checkssl\\
on Windows. The environment variables
.B CHECKSSL_CONFIG_DIR
and
.B CHECKSSL_CACHE_DIR
override those locations.
.PP
Layout:
.PP
.RS
.EX
<config dir>/
  config.toml       # defaults — see `checkssl config edit`
  domains           # primary domain list
  domains.d/        # include-globbed group files
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every setting also has an environment-variable equivalent of the form
.BR CHECKSSL_ <KEY>
(dotted keys use
.BR _ ,
e.g.
.B nagios.warning_days
→
.BR CHECKSSL_NAGIOS_WARNING_DAYS ).
Precedence from highest to lowest is: CLI flag, env var, config file,
hardcoded default. See
.B checkssl config show
to inspect the effective values and their sources.
.SH "DEFAULT DOMAIN LIST"
When neither
.B \-d
nor
.B \-f
is given,
.B checkssl
reads the files pointed to by
.B domains_file
and
.B domains_glob
in
.IR config.toml
(defaults
.I domains
and
.IR domains.d/*.list ,
both relative to the config dir). Lines beginning with
.RB \(oq # \(cq
and blank lines are ignored;
.RB \(oq # \(cq
also starts an end-of-line comment.
.PP
As a soft migration aid,
.B checkssl
still reads the legacy
.I ~/.checkssl
and
.IR ~/.domains/* .
When
.I ~/.checkssl
exists it wins outright
.RB ( either
legacy
.B or
the new layout is read, never both, so a half-migrated setup cannot
silently merge two sources). A one-shot hint on stderr points at
.BR "checkssl config migrate" .
.PP
Lines of the form
.BI "@include " path
pull in another file or a glob of files. The path may be absolute, may
start with
.RB \(oq ~/ \(cq
(expanded to the user's home directory), or is otherwise resolved
relative to the including file. Glob patterns
.RB ( * ", " ? ", " [...] )
are expanded and matches are visited in sorted order; a wildcard glob
that matches zero files is silently accepted (so an empty
.I domains.d/
is not an error); directories are silently skipped; include cycles
are detected and reported. A concrete include path with no wildcards
that does not resolve to a file is still reported as an error \(em
that is a real typo, not an empty\-collection case. Example:
.PP
.RS
.EX
# ~/.checkssl
@include ~/.domains/*.list
extra.example
.EE
.RE
.SH "OUTPUT FORMATS"
.SS table
Human-readable, three-column table with a coloured
.B "expires in"
column. Colour is emitted only when stdout is a terminal;
.B NO_COLOR=1
disables it. Legend: green > 30 days, yellow \(<= 30 days, red for
expired or invalid, dim for errors.
.SS csv
Header row
.RB \(oq Domain,Expiration,DaysUntilExpiry \(cq
followed by one row per domain.
.SS json
Pretty-printed array of objects with
.BR domain ", " expiration ", " daysUntilExpiry ", " status
and optional
.BR authorizationError " / " error
fields.
.SS html
Standalone HTML report with inline CSS, per-status toggle chips and a
live search box that filters rows against every visible cell. Pick a
style with
.BR \-\-html\-style :
.TP
.B monitor
(default) \(em dashboard look with metric tiles across the top, a
severity stripe on each row and a visual bar for days remaining.
Follows the viewer's light/dark preference.
.TP
.B terminal
monospace terminal mock with box-drawing frames and phosphor-coloured
status badges. Dark-committed; press
.RB \(oq / \(cq
anywhere to focus the filter.
.PP
Both styles are self-contained HTML files, safe to save, email or
drop into a static file server.
.SS nagios
Single-line plugin output:
.PP
.RS
.EX
STATE \- <message> | key=value key=value ...
.EE
.RE
.PP
The exit code follows the Nagios convention: 0 OK, 1 WARNING,
2 CRITICAL, 3 UNKNOWN. Overrides the default exit-code mapping
described below.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
For every format except
.BR nagios :
.TP
.B 0
Every domain came back
.B valid
or
.BR expiring_soon .
.TP
.B 1
At least one domain came back
.BR expired ", " invalid ", or " error .
.TP
.B 2
The command was invoked incorrectly (bad flag, unknown format).
.PP
For
.BR "\-\-format nagios" ,
the exit code is 0 / 1 / 2 / 3 as described above.
.SH "STATUS BUCKETS"
.TP
.B valid
Trusted certificate, more than 30 days remaining.
.TP
.B expiring_soon
Trusted certificate that expires within 30 days.
.TP
.B expired
NotAfter is in the past.
.TP
.B invalid
Certificate served but rejected: self-signed, wrong DNS name,
unknown CA, and so on. The
.B authorizationError
field carries the reason.
.TP
.B error
No certificate obtained: connection refused, timeout, DNS failure.
.SH "CONFIG SUBCOMMANDS"
.TP
.B checkssl config path
Print the resolved config dir and exit. Useful in shell scripts
.RI ( cd\  $(checkssl config path) ).
.TP
.B checkssl config edit
Open the config file in the configured editor. Creates the file (and
its parent dir) from the shipped template if it doesn't exist yet.
.TP
.B checkssl config show
Print the effective config with per-key provenance so you can see
whether a value came from the default, the file, the env, or the CLI.
.TP
.BR "checkssl config init " [ "\-\-editor " \fIname\fR ] " [\-\-non-interactive] [\-\-force]"
Create the config dir, drop a well-commented
.I config.toml
in it, and set up an empty
.I domains
file plus a
.I domains.d/
dir. With
.B --editor
pins a preset
.RI ( code ", " nvim ", " nano ", " subl ", " zed ", " hx ", …)"
or a raw template string. Without
.B --non-interactive
and on a TTY, prompts for the editor interactively.
.TP
.BR "checkssl config migrate " [ \-\-dry-run ]
Copy legacy
.I ~/.checkssl
to
.I <config dir>/domains
and every
.I ~/.domains/<name>
to
.IR "<config dir>/domains.d/<name>.list" .
Any
.BI "@include ~/.domains/" ...
directives inside the primary file are rewritten to point at the new
.I domains.d/
layout \(em without that rewrite the migrated primary would still
reference the old path, and removing the legacy
.I ~/.domains/
would leave it pointing at nothing. Each rewrite is printed to stdout
so the change can be audited. Originals are kept. With
.B --dry-run
(or
.BR \-n ),
only prints what would be copied and rewritten.
.TP
.BR "checkssl config gc " [ \-\-older-than\  \fIage\fR ]
Prune session backup folders older than the retention window. Default
is
.B tui.backup_retention
from
.IR config.toml .
.I age
may be a Go duration
.RI ( 168h )
or a plain integer meaning days
.RI ( 7 ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to any non-empty value, disables ANSI colours in the
.B table
output.
.TP
.B CHECKSSL_CONFIG_DIR
Overrides the OS-default config directory.
.TP
.B CHECKSSL_CACHE_DIR
Overrides the OS-default cache directory (where backups land).
.TP
.BR CHECKSSL_ "<KEY>"
Each setting listed by
.B checkssl config show
has an equivalent env var in SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE. Examples:
.BR CHECKSSL_TIMEOUT ,
.BR CHECKSSL_CONCURRENCY ,
.BR CHECKSSL_FORMAT ,
.BR CHECKSSL_NAGIOS_WARNING_DAYS ,
.BR CHECKSSL_EDITOR_CMD .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <config dir>/config.toml
User defaults — scan behaviour, HTML style, Nagios thresholds, TUI
options, editor template. See
.B CONFIGURATION
above.
.TP
.I <config dir>/domains
Primary domain list, one domain per line;
.RB \(oq # \(cq
starts a comment.
.TP
.I <config dir>/domains.d/
Per-group include files matched by
.BR domains_glob .
.TP
.I <cache dir>/backups/<session>/
Snapshots of files mutated in-TUI, one folder per session. Pruned by
.BR "checkssl config gc" .
.TP
.I ~/.checkssl ", " ~/.domains/
Legacy locations from pre-v2 installs. Still read as a fallback when
.I ~/.checkssl
exists (either\-or, never both);
.B checkssl config migrate
moves them and rewrites the primary's
.B @include
directives.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a single domain:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ checkssl -d example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check many domains from a file, JSON output:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ checkssl -f domains.txt --format json > report.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use as a Nagios plugin with a 60-day warn / 21-day critical policy:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ checkssl -f domains.txt \\
    --format nagios --nagios-warning 60 --nagios-critical 21
.EE
.RE
.PP
Install shell completion (fish):
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ checkssl completion fish | source
.EE
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR openssl (1),
.BR s_client (1)
